  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA pretty calm day is present across the Mid-South with current\ntemperatures in the 60s under a thin stratus deck. Highs will\nmoderate into mid 60s to mid 70s this afternoon as surface high\npressure pushes in behind this morning's cold front. Moving into\nthe overnight hours, eyes turn to increased fog potential as\nwinds go calm under clearing skies along narrow dewpoint\ndepressions. Our entire area received appreciable rainfall from\nlast night's line of storms leading to saturated soils further\naiding in fog development overnight. A Dense Fog Advisory (50-75%\nconfidence) will likely be warranted across much if not all of\nthe Mid-South as visibilities begin to plummet. Fog should begin\nto burn off shortly after sunrise as mixing occurs. We'll wake up\nto a chilly morning with lows in the 40s before we rebound\nslightly with highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s as upper-level\nsouthwest flow builds in.\n\nLate Thursday into early Friday, upper-level troughing will begin\npushing into the Mississippi Valley with an attendant reinforcing\ncold front. A cool, dry airmass will be associated with this\nfront resulting in dry conditions. Much cooler temperatures will\nbegin filtering behind this front heading into the weekend with\nhighs in the upper 50s to lower 60s and lows in the 30s and 40s.\nEarly morning frost is anticipated Saturday morning across mainly\nWest Tennessee with latest NBM probs hinting at a 45-60% chance\nof temperatures <36F. Sunday, however, will be more of a slam\ndunk for areawide frost and maybe even some freezing fog as NBM\nguidance has a 35-50% chance of freezing temperatures across\nmainly West Tennessee. Moving into next workweek, guidance is\nhinting at temperatures beginning to rebound slightly as early as\nTuesday as our next system begins to rotate into the Lower\nMississippi Valley. The latest 6-10 day outlook is in agreement\nleaning slightly above normal (40-50%) heading into midweek next\nweek. In terms of precipitation, a dry airmass looks to remain\nparked over the Middle Mississippi Valley leading to dry\nconditions over at least the next seven days.\n\nAEH",
